.TH tusysinfo n 0.1 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tusysinfo
.PP
Describe the computer: operating system, CPU, memory, model, disks and monitors, on Linux, macOS and Windows. Counterpart of \fBtuappinfo\fR, which describes the Tcl runtime. Pure Tcl, no Tk.
.PP
The module reads files and the registry first and calls a platform program only where nothing else has the data. A missing program leaves its fields empty; \fBcollect\fR does not fail because of it.
.TS
l l.
System	Sources
Linux	\fB/etc/os\-release\fR, \fB/proc/cpuinfo\fR, \fB/proc/meminfo\fR, \fB/sys/class/dmi/id\fR, \fB/sys/block\fR, \fB/sys/class/drm/*/edid\fR — no program, no root
macOS	\fBsw_vers\fR, \fBuname\fR, \fBsysctl\fR, \fBsystem_profiler \-json\fR (takes a few seconds)
Windows	registry (package \fBregistry\fR) for OS, CPU, model and monitor EDID; one PowerShell call (CIM) for memory, disks and the active monitors
.TE
.PP
Monitors are read from their EDID through \fBtclutils::tuedid\fR.
.SS Load
.CS
package require tclutils::tusysinfo 0.1
.CE
.SS API
.CS
::tclutils::tusysinfo::collect ?options?                         ;# -> dict
::tclutils::tusysinfo::report  info ?-title text? ?-anonymize 0|1? ;# -> text
::tclutils::tusysinfo::toJson  info ?-indent n?                  ;# -> JSON
.CE
.PP
Options of \fBcollect\fR:
.TS
l l l.
Option	Default	Meaning
\fB\-sections\fR	all	any of \fBos cpu memory machine disks displays\fR
\fB\-serials\fR	\fB0\fR	\fB1\fR adds serial numbers (computer, disks, monitors)
\fB\-commands\fR	\fB1\fR	\fB0\fR uses no platform program (macOS then returns nothing, Windows no memory, disks or monitor filter)
\fB\-timeout\fR	\fB10000\fR	limit in ms for one program call
\fB\-platform\fR	this computer	\fBlinux\fR, \fBmacos\fR, \fBwindows\fR
\fB\-root\fR	empty	Linux: read \fB/etc\fR, \fB/proc\fR, \fB/sys\fR below this folder (tests)
.TE
.SS Result
.CS
platform linux
host     decoder
os       {name {Debian GNU/Linux 13 (trixie)} id debian version 13 kernel 6.12.48-amd64 arch x86_64}
cpu      {model {AMD Ryzen 5 5600G} logical 12 cores 6}
memory   {total 16694710272}
machine  {vendor ASUS model ... version ... boardVendor ... board ... bios ... biosDate ...}
disks    {{name nvme0n1 model {Samsung SSD 980} size 1000204886016 type ssd removable 0} ...}
displays {{connector DP-1 manufacturer DEL vendor Dell name {DELL P2722H} productCode A0F1
           year 2022 week 10 widthCm 60 heightCm 34 diagonal 27.2
           preferred {width 1920 height 1080 refresh 60.0} valid 1 present 1} ...}
.CE
.PP
Sizes are bytes; \fBreport\fR shows disks in GB (as on the label) and memory in GiB. \fBtype\fR is \fBssd\fR, \fBhdd\fR, \fBoptical\fR or empty. On macOS \fBmachine\fR also has \fBchip\fR and \fBmemoryText\fR, \fBdisks\fR a \fBmount\fR. With \fB\-serials 1\fR \fBmachine\fR, \fBdisks\fR and \fBdisplays\fR get \fBserial\fR (monitors also \fBserialText\fR).
.SS Usage
.CS
set info [::tclutils::tusysinfo::collect]
puts [::tclutils::tusysinfo::report $info]
set fh [open sysinfo.json w]
puts $fh [::tclutils::tusysinfo::toJson $info]
close $fh
.CE
.PP
Command line: \fBtclsh bin/tusysinfo.tcl ?\-\-json? ?\-\-serials? ?\-\-anonymize?\fR (\fBtusysinfo(1)\fR).
.SS Notes
.IP \(bu
Serial numbers, host names and MAC addresses identify a computer and, on an office PC, its user. Serials come only with \fB\-serials 1\fR; \fBreport \-anonymize 1\fR hides the host name and serials. MAC addresses are not collected.
.IP \(bu
Linux: the computer's serial number (\fBproduct_serial\fR) is readable by root only; it stays empty otherwise. Virtual machines often have no DMI data and no monitor.
.IP \(bu
Monitors carry \fBpresent\fR: 1 connected, 0 not connected, empty when unknown. Windows keeps every monitor that was ever connected, often the same one under several instances (other port, other driver); \fBtusysinfo\fR lists each EDID once and takes \fBpresent\fR from \fBGet\-PnpDevice \-Class Monitor\fR (status OK). In a remote desktop session the physical monitors count as not connected. \fBreport\fR marks them "(not connected)". (\fBWmiMonitorID\fR is not used: it reports "not supported" on some systems.) Windows 11 still reports \fBProductName\fR "Windows 10 …"; a build of 22000 or later is shown as Windows 11. SSD or HDD comes from \fBGet\-PhysicalDisk\fR (an NVMe disk counts as SSD).
.IP \(bu
Firmware placeholders such as "Default string" or "To be filled by O.E.M." are left empty.
.IP \(bu
macOS: \fBsystem_profiler\fR is slow (several seconds); \fB\-sections {os cpu memory}\fR avoids it.
.IP \(bu
The macOS and Windows parts are tested with constructed program output and a simulated registry, not yet on real Macs and Windows PCs.
.IP \(bu
Program calls run with \fBvwait\fR; in a Tk application the event loop runs while waiting.
.SS Errors
.PP
\fB{TCLUTILS TUSYSINFO OPTION}\fR unknown option or missing value, \fB{TCLUTILS TUSYSINFO SECTION}\fR unknown section, \fB{TCLUTILS TUSYSINFO PLATFORM}\fR unknown platform.
